%N A399607 Level of n in a poset graph that assigns the next lower level to a divisor <= sqrt(n).
%C A399607 One can start with the two conceptionally "most difficult" (or cryptographically most secure, most balanced) divisors of n, one A033676(n), the other A033677(n). This defines a poset of the positive integers if we define the level of n to be one plus the level of A033676(n), assigning a level of 1 to n = 1 or prime.
%C A399607 The first 4's appear at a(256,272,288,304,320,336,...)=4. The first 5's appear at a(65536, 65792, 66048, 66304,...)=5.
%C A399607 [The concept is vaguely similar to A083647 or A067029 in descending from n to a selected set of divisors.]
%F A399607 a(n) = 1 if n in A008578, else a(n) = 1+a(A033676(n)) .
%p A399607 A399607 := proc(n)
%p A399607     option remember ;
%p A399607     if n = 1 or isprime(n) then
%p A399607         return 1;
%p A399607     else
%p A399607         1+procname(A033676(n)) ;
%p A399607     end if;
%p A399607 end proc:
%p A399607 seq(A399607(n),n=1..120) ;
%Y A399607 Cf. A033676.
%K A399607 nonn,easy,less,new
%O A399607 1,4
%A A399607 _R. J. Mathar_, Sep 04 2026
